== A Wife’s Not Going Against Her Husband’s Will (I)

=== §. 17. #emph[Of a wife’s obedience in general.]

Thus far we have spoken of a wife’s #emph[reverence;] next we must speak of her #emph[obedience.] The first law that was ever given to woman since her fall laid this duty of #emph[obedience] to her husband on her, in these words: “Your desire will be to your husband, and he will rule over you.”#footnote[Gen 3:16.] How can a husband rule over a wife if she does not obey him? The principal part of that #emph[submission] which is required of a wife in this #footnote[Eph 5:22.] text, and in many other places, consists in #emph[obedience.] And therefore it is expressly commended to wives in the example of Sarah, who #footnote[1 Pet 3:6 ὑπήκουσε.] “obeyed Abraham.” In the same way, it is by #emph[obedience] that the church shows her #emph[subjection] to Christ.

The #emph[place] in which God has set a husband, namely, to be a #emph[head;]#footnote[Eph 5:23.] the #emph[authority] which he has given him, to be a #emph[lord]#footnote[1 Pet 3:6.] and #emph[master;]#footnote[Esth 1:17.] the #emph[duty] which he requires of him, #footnote[Gen 3:16.] to #emph[rule:] all these require #emph[obedience] of a wife. Is not obedience to be yielded to a #emph[head, lord] and #emph[master?] You#super[+] take away all authority from a husband if you#super[+] exempt a wife from obedience.

Contrary to this is the stubbornness of such wives as must have their #footnote[Contrary to obedience, stubbornness.] own will and do as they please, or else there will be no quiet in the house. #emph[Their] will must be done; #emph[they] must rule and overrule everything; #emph[they] must command not only children and servants, but husbands also, if the husband at least wants to have peace. Look into families, observe the state and condition of many of them, and then tell me if these things are not so. If a husband is a man of courage, and seeks to stand on his right and maintain his authority by requiring obedience of his wife, it is strange to see what a hurly-burly she will make in the house. But if he is a milksop, and weakly gives in to his wife and allows her to rule, then perhaps there will be some outward quiet. The ground of this is an ambitious and proud disposition in women, who insist on ruling, or else they think themselves slaves. But let them think what they like: in this they certainly thwart God’s ordinance, pervert the order of nature, deface the image of Christ, overthrow the ground of all duty, hinder the good of the family, become a bad pattern to children and servants, lay themselves open to Satan, and incur many other harms which cannot but follow from violating this main duty of #emph[obedience.] If this is not performed, how can other duties be expected?

=== §. 18. #emph[Of the cases in which a wife has power to order things of the house without her husband’s consent.]

A wife’s #emph[obedience] requires

- #emph[Submission.]
- #emph[Contentment.]

#emph[Submission] in yielding to her husband’s mind and will.

#emph[Contentment]#footnote[See §. 49.] in resting satisfied and content with his condition and means.

That #emph[submission] consists in two things.

First, in abstaining from doing things against her husband’s mind.

Secondly, in doing what her husband requires. The first of these requires that a wife have her husband’s consent for the things which she does. To make this clearer, we must consider:

1\. What kind of husbands they must be whose consent is required.

2\. In how many ways his consent may be given.

3\. What the things are about which his consent is to be looked for.

As for the first: on the one side it often happens that a wise, #footnote[In two cases a wife has power to order things by herself, without her husband’s consent.] provident and religious man is married to a foolish woman, an utter idiot who has no understanding, and of her there can be no question that such a wife is to do nothing by herself and on her own initiative, but is to be wholly ordered by her husband. So on the other side it often happens that a wise, virtuous and gracious woman is married to a husband destitute #footnote[1\. In case of her husband’s incapacity.] of understanding: to a born fool (as we say), or a madman, or to one made very dull and stupid, unfit to manage his affairs, through some disorder, wound or sickness. In such a case the whole government lies on the wife, so that her husband’s consent is not to be waited for.

#emph[Question.] What if the husband is a wicked and profane man, and so blinded and stupefied in his soul? Does not this spiritual blindness and dullness give a religious wife as much liberty as natural stupidity does?

#emph[Answer.] Truly, no. For Peter exhorts believing wives #footnote[1 Pet 3:1, 2.] who were married to unbelieving husbands “to be subject” to them, and that “in fear.”

The reason is clear. For spiritual blindness does not disqualify a man from civil government. Indeed, nothing that such a man does is acceptable to God, or of any avail to his own salvation; but yet it may be profitable to man. A wicked man may be provident enough for wife, children and the whole family in outward, temporal things.

Again, it often happens that a husband is #footnote[In case it is impossible for her husband to do it.] far away from the house for a long time: sometimes because of his calling, as an ambassador, soldier or sailor; sometimes also carelessly or willfully neglecting house, goods, wife, children and all. And in his absence he has left no instructions for ordering things at home. In this case also there is no question that the wife has power to manage matters without her husband’s consent, provided that she observes those rules of God’s word concerning justice, equity, truth and mercy which a husband ought to observe in managing them.

The first of these cases shows an #emph[incapacity] in the husband; the other an #emph[impossibility] for him to order matters. Therefore, since the wife is next to the husband, the power of ordering things passes to her: she is not bound to have his consent.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of various kinds of consent.]

#smallcaps[II]. A consent may be #emph[general] or #emph[particular.] A general #footnote[1\. A general consent.] consent is given when, without specific reference to this or that particular thing, liberty is granted to a wife by her husband to do all things as seems good in her own eyes. #footnote[Prov 31:10, #emph[etc.]] That excellent good wife and notable good housewife who is set forth by the wise man had such a consent. For first it is said, “The heart of her husband trusts in her”;#footnote[#emph[v.] 11.] and then it is concluded that she orders all the things of the house, many particulars of which are specified there. From this I gather that her husband, seeing her to be a godly, wise, faithful and industrious woman, gave her power and liberty to do what she thought good in the household affairs (he being a public magistrate, for “he is known at the city gate, where he sits among the elders of the land”),#footnote[#emph[v.] 23.] and that she used her liberty accordingly.

A particular consent is one which is given to one or more particular #footnote[2\. A particular consent.] things, such as the consent #footnote[Gen 16:6.] which Abraham gave to Sarah about Hagar, and #footnote[1 Sam 1:23.] the one which Elkanah gave to Hannah about staying at home till her child was weaned.

This particular consent may be #emph[expressed] or #emph[implied.] An expressed consent is when the husband makes known his approval by word, writing, message or sign, and that whether his consent is asked (as was noted in the example of Elkanah) or freely offered.

An implicit consent is when it may be gathered by any likely inference that the husband’s will is not against such a thing, #footnote[4\. An implicit consent.] though he has not made known his mind about that very particular. This implicit consent may be gathered either from his silence when he is present to see a thing done, or otherwise knows of it; or else, when he is absent, from his past conduct or disposition in other similar cases. The Scripture counts a husband’s silence to be a consent when he knows of a thing and could forbid it, but does not: as #footnote[Num 30:8.] in the case of a wife’s vow. For all we read to the contrary, #footnote[2 Kgs 4:10, 23.] the Shunammite had no other consent to prepare a room for the prophet, and to go to him, than her husband’s silence, and his not forbidding it when he knew of it.

As for the other kind of implicit consent, it may be set out in the following instance. Suppose a good wife has a husband whom she knows, from his past conduct and disposition, to be a compassionate and charitable man, who takes every occasion to show mercy; and in his absence a fit and needful occasion of showing mercy arises. If she takes that occasion to show mercy, she has an implicit consent, for she may well think that if her husband knew of it he would approve of what she does. We may suppose that Hannah#footnote[1 Sam 1:11.] vowed her child to God on some such ground. For it is not likely that she who would not stay at home to wean her child without her husband’s consent would vow him to the Lord (which was a far greater matter) without some persuasion that her husband approved of it. Now, so that a wife may show that she deals uprightly in this case, from a true persuasion of her heart about her husband’s mind, she ought (when she reasonably can) to make known to her husband what she has done: as Hannah without any question did. And this much may be gathered from the words which Elkanah spoke to Hannah: “May the #smallcaps[Lord] confirm His word.”#footnote[1 Sam 1:23.]

=== §. 20. #emph[Of the things about which a wife must have her husband’s consent.]

The things about which a husband’s consent is to be looked for are such as he has power to order by virtue of his place and authority: for example, ordering and disposing of the goods, livestock, servants and children of the family, and receiving strangers; yes, even ordering his wife’s going out of the house, and her making of vows, and the like. Now then, to set out distinctly a wife’s duty in this first branch of obedience, it is this:

#emph[A wife must do nothing which belongs to her husband’s authority wholly without, or directly against, his consent.] Do not these words of that old law (“Your desire will be to your husband”) #footnote[Gen 3:16.] imply as much? I do not deny that there may be various things that belong to a wife as her own, in which I will not restrain her liberty; and therefore I use this phrase, #emph[(which belongs to her husband’s authority.)] And I grant that the general and implicit consent named above is a true consent, so that there is no need of an express, particular consent for everything; and therefore I have added these clauses, #emph[(wholly without, or directly against consent.)]

That is done #emph[wholly without consent] which is done without any warrant from the husband, and so secretly that she is afraid it should come to his notice, imagining that he would by no means like it: as Rachel’s taking her father’s idols#footnote[Gen 31:32.] without any consent of Jacob.

That is done #emph[directly against consent] which is expressly forbidden and disallowed by the husband.

But to come down to the particulars mentioned before. First, concerning the goods of the family: it is a disputed question whether the wife has power to dispose of them without or against the husband’s consent.

Before I settle the question, I think it needful to declare 1. what goods, and 2. what occasion of giving, the question is about.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of the things which a wife may dispose of without her husband’s consent.]

1\. As for the goods, some belong to the wife as her own; #footnote[#emph[Quaedam bona sunt propria uxoris, vt sunt bona paraphernalia. Greg. Sayr. Clau. reg. l.] 9. #emph[c.] 16. \[Some goods are the wife’s own, as are paraphernal goods.\]] others are common. Goods that are the wife’s own are those which, before marriage, she herself or her family reserve from the husband for her sole and personal use and disposal, and to which he also agrees; or those which, after marriage, he gives her to dispose of as she pleases: say some rent, annuity, fees, gratuities, or the like.

These kinds of goods are excluded from the question #footnote[Παράφερνα #emph[bona apud iurisconsultos, dicuntur] τὰ ἐξώπροικα #emph[(i.) quae sponsa praeter dotem à parentibus pactam secum fert: vel (vt Sayrus loc. citat.) habet legata ei, seu donata.] \[Among the lawyers, paraphernal goods are called “things beyond the dowry,” that is, what the bride brings with her besides the dowry agreed by her parents; or (as Sayrus, in the place cited) what she has had bequeathed or given to her.\]] at hand: the wife has liberty to dispose of them as she pleases without any further consent than she had by virtue of her husband’s former grant.

To these I may add other goods, but of another nature: namely, such as someone close to her, say her father, mother, brother or anyone else, seeing her husband to be a very hard man, who does not allow enough for herself, much less to give to charitable uses, gives her to dispose of as she pleases, charging her not to let her husband know of it. Now because it is in the power of a free donor to order his gift as he pleases, and because he orders this gift in such a way that he will not have her husband know of it, I do not doubt that she may dispose of such goods by herself, according to the donor’s mind, without her husband’s consent. In this she is only like a trustee.

Again, of the common goods, some are set aside by the husband to be spent on the family; others he reserves as a stock, or to lay out as he himself sees occasion.

Concerning those which are set aside to be spent, I do not doubt that the wife has power to dispose of them; nor is she bound to ask any further consent of her husband. For it is the place and duty of wives to guide, or “govern their households,” #footnote[1 Tim 5:14 οἰκοδεσποτεῖν.] and by virtue of this a wife, providing sufficiently for the family, may give from such goods as are set apart to be spent to the poor, or otherwise, as she sees good occasion.

I have noted this for those tender consciences who think they cannot give a bit of bread or a scrap of food to a poor person, or make a dish of broth or a hot drink for a sick person, unless they first ask their husband’s consent.

This is provided that, if her husband expressly forbids this liberty, she does not take it unless necessity requires it.

But our question concerns such goods as the husband has not set apart, but has reserved to his own disposal.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of a wife’s liberty in extraordinary matters.]

#smallcaps[II]. As for the #emph[occasion] of disposing of goods, it may be ordinary or extraordinary. Extraordinary, for the good of the husband himself and others in the family, or of those who are outside the family. If an extraordinary occasion arises in which the wife, by disposing of the goods without or against her husband’s consent, may bring a great good to the family, or prevent and keep a great injury from it, she is not to wait for his consent: take as an instance the example of Abigail.#footnote[1 Sam 25:18. #emph[etc.]] So a faithful, provident wife who sees her husband given to wild living, spending all he can get on cards, dice and drink, may without his consent lay up what goods she can for her husband’s, her own, her children’s and the whole household’s good. This is no part of disobedience, but a point in which she may show herself a very good help to her husband: #footnote[Gen 2:18.] the end for which a wife was first made.

Concerning those who are outside the family: if they are in great need and require immediate relief, then, though the wife knows her husband to be so hard-hearted that he will not allow her to relieve such a person, yet she may relieve him without his consent. The ground of this and other such cases is the rule laid down by the prophets and by Christ himself, namely, “I desire mercy, not sacrifice.”#footnote[Hos 6:6. Matt 9:13; 12:7.] If God waives a duty owed to himself in a case of mercy, will he not much more waive a duty owed to a husband?

=== §. 23. #emph[Of a wife’s restraint in disposing of goods without her husband’s consent, and of the ground of that restraint.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 54.]

From all these things set down beforehand, I gather the true state #footnote[Non potest vxor ex bonis communibus occulte accipere ad donandum vel ad erogandum in alios vsus sine expressa, aut praesumpta saltem viri licentia. Greg. Sayr. loc. citat. \[A wife cannot secretly take from the common goods to give away, or to spend on other uses, without her husband’s express, or at least presumed, leave.\]] of the question in dispute, concerning the power of wives in disposing of the goods of the family, to be this:

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Whether a wife may, secretly and wholly without, or openly and directly against, her husband’s consent, give away such common goods of the family as her husband reserves to his own disposal, when there is no extraordinary necessity?]]

The most ancient and common answer to this question has been negative, namely, that a wife does not have power to do so; and for my part I subscribe to it.

The ground of this answer is taken from that primary law of the wife’s subjection: “Your desire will be to your husband.”#footnote[Gen 3:16.] How is her desire subject to her husband if, in the case put forward, she does not wait on his consent? It is further confirmed both by the #footnote[§. 17.] proofs named above and by all the other proofs that might be produced out of the Scripture concerning the subjection of wives to their husbands. If she does not yield subjection in ordering the goods of the family, in what will she yield it?

Against this foundation some object that #footnote[Gen 4:7.] the same law of subjection is laid on a younger brother in the very same words, and yet a younger brother was not bound by it #footnote[How the law of subjection is applied to younger brothers.] to have his elder brother’s consent in disposing of his goods.

#emph[Answer.] The law of the royal right (if I may so speak) and preeminence of the #emph[first-born] was ordained under those words; and therefore a younger brother was made a subject to his elder while he remained in the family, as a son to the father. The elder brother was like a lord over his other brothers. That is why, when Isaac conferred the right of the first-born on Jacob (thinking he was his eldest son Esau), he used these words: “Be lord over your brothers, and may the #footnote[Gen 27:29.] sons of your mother bow down to you.” This being so, without question the younger brother could not dispose of the goods of the family wholly without or directly against the elder brother’s consent. So this objection establishes the argument named above all the more strongly.

Again, it is objected that that old law is to be understood of weighty matters.

#emph[Answer.] The apostle,#footnote[Eph 5:24.] who was guided by the spirit of the lawgiver, extends that law to #emph[everything.] But is not this matter of disposing of goods a weighty matter? The consequences which I will shortly note as following from it will show it to be a matter of importance.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the example of the Shunammite in asking her husband’s consent.] #footnote[2 Kgs 4:9, 22.]

As another reason, the Shunammite’s pattern may be cited: she asked her husband’s consent before she prepared the things that were thought fitting for receiving the prophet, and before she used the things that were fitting for her journey.

#emph[Objection.] It is indeed commendable for wives to seek their husbands’ consent as she did; but where such consent cannot be had, it is not necessary.

#emph[Answer.] Since this example is grounded on a law (as we showed #footnote[§. 23.] before), it declares not only what may be done, but also what ought to be done. And if a wife is bound to have her husband’s consent for doing a thing, it follows by consequence that she is barred from doing it without her husband’s consent.

2\. #emph[Answer.] Those who object to this reason taken from example use the same kind of reason themselves on other points, such as the examples of Abigail, Joanna and Susanna for the contrary.

2\. #emph[Objection.] In the Shunammite’s example there was more than a merciful relief of the prophet, namely, bringing him into the house to eat and to lodge, in which the husband must have the chief say.

#emph[Answer.] The word of God makes no such difference between relieving and receiving: it extends a wife’s subjection to #emph[everything.] Therefore the husband has the chief say in the one as well as in the other.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of the law of a wife’s vow.]

A third reason is taken from the law of a wife’s vow,#footnote[Num 30:7. #emph[etc.]] by which it is implied in general that a wife could not make a vow without her husband’s consent. From this it follows, as an argument from the greater to the less, that she must not dispose of the goods without his consent. Yes, #footnote[#emph[v.] 13.] the law further says expressly that, though she has vowed, yet her husband has power to annul her vow. Note here how the Lord will rather give up his own right (if I may so speak) than have the order which he has appointed between man and woman broken. The Lord’s right was to have what was vowed to him performed; the order which he appointed was to have the wife subject to her husband. Rather than have the wife do what the husband would not have done, the Lord released a wife from her vow in case her husband would not consent to have it performed. Now then I ask: is the disposing of goods a greater matter than the performing of a vow? Or does a wife have more liberty in these days than in former days? If she does, by what law? Was there ever, under the law, a stricter charge laid on wives than this: “Let wives be subject to their husbands in everything”?

#emph[Objection.] That point of a woman’s subjection in performing her vow is a particular case expressly ruled, but this of disposing of goods is not.

#emph[Answer.] By particular laws and examples the Scripture gives directions for other cases like them; and arguments drawn by just and necessary consequence are counted as sound as express testimonies. As for the claim that this particular in question is not expressly decided, I take the reason for it to be this: in former times they marked the extent of the general law of a wife’s subjection so well that it never came into question with them to do this or other such things without their husbands’ consent. Good wives did not take that liberty, nor did that liberty have any defenders.

2\. #emph[Objection.] The case of a wife’s disposing of goods is unlike that of vows, because vows are voluntary, but disposing of goods, as a work of mercy, is necessary.

#emph[Answer.] Though it was a voluntary thing to make or not to make a vow, yet once a vow was made, it was not in the power of the person who made it not to perform it: it was a #footnote[Num 30:3. Deut 23:21. Eccl 5:4.] necessary duty to perform a vow, even expressly commanded. As for the alleged work of mercy, I will #footnote[§. 34.] show hereafter that a wife is not necessarily bound to it.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of human laws which restrain wives from disposing of goods without or against their husbands’ consent.]

A fourth reason is taken from the laws of men, to which we are subject, and which we must obey even for conscience’ sake, so far as they do not thwart God’s law; and in this case they do not, as the reasons gathered before out of God’s word show.

Now our law says that #footnote[45\. #emph[E.] 3. #emph[Fitz. Coven.] 18.] every gift, grant or disposal of goods, lands or anything else whatever made by a married #footnote[1\. #emph[H.] 5. 12. #emph[b. Perk. cap. de Grant. Nat. Breu. fol.] 120. #emph[G.]] woman, and each and every bond and conveyance of land made by her, and recovery allowed by her, are void if they are done without her husband’s consent. Yes, #footnote[#emph[Nat. Breu. fol.] 188. #emph[H.]] if she does wrong to another, she has nothing with which to make satisfaction while she is married: either her husband must make it, or it must be made by the imprisonment of her person. And #footnote[#emph[Perk. fol.] 2. #emph[a.]] though she has an inheritance of her own, yet she cannot grant any annuity out of it while she is married, without her husband; if any deed is made to that purpose without his consent, or in her name alone, it is void in law. Yes, #footnote[#emph[Perk. fol.] 2. #emph[b.]] if there is a dispute between the husband and his wife, by which certain lands of the husband’s are assigned to the wife with his consent, and she grants an annuity to a stranger out of such lands, the grant is void. And #footnote[27\. #emph[H.] 8. 27. #emph[p.] 12.] if he covenants to give her such and such clothing yearly, she cannot dispose of it as she pleases without his consent, but may only use and wear it herself. #footnote[#emph[Perk. fol.] 3. #emph[a.]] Nor can she lease her own land for years, for life, etc. If she does, it is void, and the lessee who enters by force of it wrongfully dispossesses the husband, and is a trespasser. And #footnote[21\. #emph[H.] 7. 18. #emph[p.] 29. #emph[Nat. Breu. fol.] 120 #emph[G.]] if she sells anything, the sale is void, unless she is a merchant, where custom enables her to trade. Finally, #footnote[#emph[Ognels] case, #emph[Coke] Rep. 4. 3 3. #emph[Deu.] 12.] she cannot make executors without the consent of her husband, nor a devise of land, or a will. #footnote[#emph[Plowd. Com.] 344. #emph[a. Bret] and #emph[Rigdons] case.] If she makes a will and leaves her own inheritance by it, and her husband dies, and she dies afterward without publishing it anew, it is of no force, because it was void from the first. These and many other such cases which might be cited plainly show that by law a wife does not have power of herself, without her husband, to dispose of the common goods of the family.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of the harms which may follow from a wife’s disposing of goods without or against her husband’s consent.]

A fifth reason may be taken from the harms which would #footnote[1\. A man’s wealth may be wasted before he can tell how to remedy it.] follow if this liberty were given to women. They are these:

1\. The wealth of the family might be wasted before any remedy could be thought of. For if the wife may dispose of the goods without her husband’s consent, it must also be granted that she may do it without his knowledge; for it is to be supposed that if he knew of the disposing of what he does not like, he would hinder it. And if it is done without his knowledge, then what he thinks is remaining as a stock for the family may be laid out by the wife, and nothing left; whereas if he knew of the spending of that stock, he might perhaps be more thrifty and sparing in other expenses.

#emph[Objection.] This liberty is not granted to wives beyond their husbands’ means.

#emph[Answer.] Wives cannot always know their husbands’ means. For a husband may be deeply in debt, and yet, to maintain his credit, by which he hopes to raise his fortunes, he may allow a liberal provision for his house. If his wife then gathers from this that he is very rich, and is accordingly very bountiful in her gifts, she may soon go beyond his means, and so increase his debt that he will never be able to recover himself.

2\. Persons of contrary religions and dispositions who are #footnote[2\. Those who profess contrary religions may be supported by the goods of the same family.] outside the family might be maintained by the goods of the same family. For if the husband were of one religion and the wife of another, he might maintain those of his religion without her knowledge, and she might maintain those of her religion without his knowledge.

#emph[Objection.] This liberty of disposing of goods given to the wife is limited within the bounds of the household of faith.

#emph[Answer.] If divines grant wives this liberty, the wives will judge and determine for themselves who are of the household of faith. Popish wives will say (say what we can to the contrary) that Jesuits, priests and friars are of the household of faith, and its principal members.

3\. Many quarrels and contentions would arise from it between #footnote[3\. Quarrels would arise between man and wife.] husband and wife. For if a wife persists in doing what her husband will not consent to, surely not one husband in a thousand will bear it well, but will rather seek every way he can to cross her, thinking himself despised if she has her way whether he will or not.

#emph[Objection.] Wives must use this liberty with all due respect for their husbands’ authority.

#emph[Answer.] If the husband firmly stands on his authority, and in spite of all the fair means that can be used will not grant this liberty, I do not know what better respect she can show to this authority than to refrain and abstain from doing what she would otherwise most gladly do. But if, when it comes to the last point, she says it is her right, and that if she cannot have his consent she will do it without his consent, she shows no great respect in this.

Many other harms might be listed, but I will not dwell on them any longer. Only, from these let it be well considered whether it would not be better for a family that the husband should be barred from disposing of the goods without his wife’s consent (so that there might be, according to the proverb, only #footnote[Though she had ownership, yet she does not on that account have liberty to dispose of the goods.] one hand in the purse) than that both husband and wife should have liberty to dispose of them without each other’s consent.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of ownership of goods, whether it gives a wife liberty to dispose of them as she will.]

To justify a wife’s liberty in disposing of the common goods #footnote[#emph[Licet vxor proprietatem habeat, administrationem tamen, & vsumfructum bonorum viuente marito non habet. Greg. Sayr. clau. reg. lib.] 9. #emph[cap.] 16. \[Though a wife has the ownership, yet while her husband lives she does not have the management and the use of the goods.\]] of the family without her husband’s consent, it is said that she has a true right and ownership in those goods.

1\. #emph[Answer.] Even if it were granted that a wife has true ownership of the goods, this conclusion would still not follow from it, that she has power of herself to dispose of the goods without her husband’s consent. For the authority which God has given a husband, and the subjection which he has laid on a wife, restrain her power and liberty in what is her own. For example, suppose #footnote[See #emph[Doctr. & stud. fol.] 13.] that a woman at the time of her marriage has a lease for years, or the wardship of the body and lands of a minor, or has it by gift or #footnote[#emph[Perk. plito.] 560. 26. #emph[H.] 8. 7. #emph[p.] 1. #emph[Plowd. comment.] 418. #emph[b.]] purchase after marriage: she cannot give it away, however extreme the need may be, but her husband may dispose of it at any time during the marriage, and his disposing of it will cut off the wife’s claim. Or suppose that the only child of her father is an heiress of land, and has in herself (her father being dead) the full possession of it; or that a widow has the right to, and possession of, her husband’s estate, and, so possessed, is married to a husband: does she, being a wife, have liberty to dispose of that estate which she brought with her #emph[without] or #emph[against her husband’s consent?] I think no one will say so. I am sure that whatever she gives, lends, sells or otherwise disposes of without his consent, he may, if he will, recover again for his lifetime; and yet no man will deny that she has the truest interest and ownership in the lands and inheritance named above.

#emph[Objection.] May she not dispose of her own inheritance as well as of those goods or revenues which her husband gives her?

#emph[Answer.] No; for the husband’s gift is a general consent of his for her to dispose of what is given her as she sees fit.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of the reasons against a wife’s ownership of the common goods of the family.]

2\. #emph[Answer.] It may safely be denied that a wife has ownership of the common goods of the family, of which she is not an heir. For ownership of goods is a civil matter, and is to be limited according to the law of man under which we live. Where the law or custom of the place makes all the children coheirs, all have an equal right to their separate parts; where the eldest only is made heir, he has a right to all; where the youngest only is made heir, he has a right to all; but neither the law of nations nor the law of the land where we live gives the wife ownership. #footnote[12\. #emph[H.] 7. 22. #emph[Coke rep.] 5. #emph[fo.] 36. 28. #emph[H.] 6. #emph[Dier fol.] 6. #emph[plito.] 5 #emph[Doct. & stud. fo.] 13. #emph[Plowd. Comment. Bracebriges] Case.] By the common law marriage is a gift of all the goods and personal chattels of the wife to her husband, so that no kind of ownership of them remains in her. #footnote[4\. #emph[H.] 6. 31.] And all personal goods and chattels given to the wife during marriage are at once, #emph[ipso facto,] transferred (as to the ownership of them) to the husband. So by our law she is so far from gaining any ownership in her husband’s goods by her marriage that she loses all the ownership she formerly had in her own goods. Yes, #footnote[18\. #emph[E.] 4. 11. #emph[p.] 4.] even her necessary clothing is not hers to own. While she remains a wife she is (to use the law’s phrase) #emph[under covert baron,] that is, under her husband’s covering. #footnote[See §. 26.] She can neither lease, sell, alienate, give, nor otherwise rightfully make anything over, no, nor even make a will to dispose of any goods, while her husband lives, without his consent: which a husband may yet do while his wife lives, and that without or against her consent.

#emph[Objection.] The law gives a wife a right in a great part of the husband’s goods, providing for her jointure or her widow’s third, which the husband cannot dispose of without her consent.

#emph[Answer.] This provision is only for the time of her widowhood, in case she outlives him; but for the time that she remains his wife he may dispose of everything, and she can recover none of it till he is dead.

#emph[Objection.] This restraint of wives holds only in the court of men.

#emph[Answer.] Since it is not against the law of God, it must also hold good in the court of conscience. More than that, it agrees with the law of God and is grounded on it.

For (to leave out the proofs cited before) what could be the reason that the daughters of Zelophehad, who were heirs to their father, were forbidden to marry outside their father’s tribe, and that a law was made that no daughters who possessed any inheritance should marry outside their father’s tribe, except that all that a woman had before marriage passed to the husband and became his by virtue of marriage? This also is noted for that purpose by some, not unfittingly nor without probability: that it is the common phrase of Scripture to call husbands #footnote[#emph[Si quis abduxerit vxorem cum bonis familiae dicitur abducere vxorem cum bonis viri. Fitzherb. Nat. breuium fol.] 89. \[If anyone carries off a wife with the goods of the family, he is said to carry off the wife with the husband’s goods.\]] (but not wives) #emph[rich,] implying by this that riches belong to husbands as their own. Yes, usually in Scripture goods and lands are said to be the husband’s.

#emph[Objection.] The wives of Jacob call the goods which their husband had #emph[theirs,] saying, “all the wealth that God has taken away from our father is OURS,” Gen 31:16.

#emph[Answer.] They use the word #emph[ours] in opposition to their father’s house, and in relation not to their persons but to their #footnote[Ut vinum temperatum licet maiori ex parte aquae constat, vinum appellamus: sic census quamuis plus contribuerit vxor, nuncupandus viri est. Plut. in praec. connub.] husband’s family; and therefore they add “and our children’s.” So by that place no greater right can be proved for wives than for children. When the Holy Spirit speaks of the same goods, he does not say, in relation to both husband and wives, #emph[their] flocks, #emph[their] substance, but only in relation to the husband, #emph[his] flocks, his substance. For as in a mixture of wine and water, though the greater quantity is water, yet we call the whole #emph[wine,] so in the common goods of the family, though the wife should bring the greater part, we call it all #emph[the husband’s.]

=== §. 30. #emph[Of answers to the reasons for a wife’s ownership.]

To prove a wife’s ownership of the common goods of the family, the following reasons are alleged.

1\. #emph[Objection.] Since marriage gives a wife a right to her husband’s body, #footnote[1 Cor 7:4.] much more does it give her a right to his goods.

#emph[Answer.] I deny the consequence. For the use of the body is an act belonging only to the marriage bond, in which the difference between superiority and subjection does not appear: the wife has as great a power over the husband’s body as the husband over the wife’s. That is not so with the goods: not one thing can be named in which the power and authority of the husband consists more than in the goods.

2\. #emph[Objection.] In the form of marriage the man says to his wife, “With all my worldly goods I thee endow.”

#emph[Answer.] 1. Those words are to be understood of the use of his goods, and not of ownership of them.

2\. If a husband intends ownership by them, the ownership which she has by them she has not by virtue of the general law of marriage, but by his particular free gift.

3\. Those words are not used in the form of marriage in all countries. If those words give the wife her ownership, then wives who are married without those words being used have no ownership; so this cannot be a general ground of liberty for all wives.

3\. #emph[Objection.] A wife has as good a right in her husband’s goods as the church has in Christ’s blood; but there the church has ownership.

#emph[Answer.] Neither of those points can be proved. But if a wife’s right in her husband’s goods is like the church’s in Christ’s blood, what is gained by it? The church does not have power to dispose of his blood without or against Christ’s consent: the Church of Rome is counted a proud, usurping harlot for taking it on herself to do so.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of the privileges of wives above children and servants in and about the goods of the family.]

#emph[Question.] Where then is the wife’s preferment above servants and children, if she does not have ownership?

I answer, “Much in every way.”

1\. There#footnote[Treatise 4, §. 52, 54.] is due to her a freer and more plentiful use of all the goods than to them.

2\. By her place she has the ordering and disposing of the goods allotted for the common use of the family, as was #footnote[§. 21.] granted before.

3\. Her husband ought to give her a portion to dispose of as she sees good, #footnote[Treatise 4, §. 54.] as we will show later, when we come to the husband’s duties.

4\. She is a joint governor with her husband over the children and servants, as was shown #footnote[§. 4.] before.

Again I answer that this argument might as well be brought against that fear, subjection and obedience which the Scripture expressly requires of wives; and it might be asked, if wives must fear and obey their husbands and be subject to them, where is their preferment above their children and servants? But #footnote[§. 11.] it has been shown that, though the same things in substance are required of wives as are required of children and servants, yet there is a great difference in the manner of performing them.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of examples and other reasons alleged for the liberty of wives to dispose of goods.]

2\. Abigail’s example is alleged for a wife’s liberty, and #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason. Examples.] 1 Sam 25:18. #emph[etc.] Prov 31:11, #emph[etc.]] the example of the good housewife described by Solomon.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Abigail’s example was extraordinary. Besides, who can tell whether her husband’s heart did not so trust in her that he committed the whole government of the house to her, so that she had a general consent for what she did?

2\. It is clear that the other good wife had her husband’s consent for what she did. For besides its being said that “the heart of her husband trusts in her,” it is also said that “her husband praises her.” Therefore he was neither ignorant of what she did nor unwilling that she should do it: it was neither without nor against his consent.

3\. It is alleged that wives take as much care in getting #footnote[3\. #emph[Reason.] Wives have a like care in getting goods.] goods, or in preserving them for the good of the family; therefore it is just and fair that they should have a like power in disposing of them.

#emph[Answer.] Though the first part may be questioned, at least for the greater sort and number of wives, yet to answer this reason I need not question it; for the consequence does not follow, even if that is granted. The right of disposing of goods does not arise wholly from the care and pains of getting and preserving them, but from the order which the Lord has been pleased to set down. A wise and industrious child may be a means to raise and increase his father’s estate when his father takes little care and pains about it. Yes, a faithful and wise steward or other servant (as Jacob and Joseph were) may do much more by his pains and care in getting and preserving the goods of the family than his master; yet it will not follow from this that such a child or such a servant has as great a right and power to dispose of such goods as his father or his master.

4\. The close union between man and wife is alleged: #footnote[4\. #emph[Reason.] Wives are yokefellows with their husbands.] they are said to be yokefellows, and from this it is inferred that they have a like power in disposing of goods.

#emph[Answer.] They are yokefellows in mutual fellowship, not in equal authority; and in relation to others, such as children and servants, not in opposition to each other. In this respect she is subject, not equal. If therefore he wills one thing and she another, she must not think to have an equal right and power: she must give place and yield.
